      <h2>Migration checklist</h2>
      <p>The scenarios and deep dives on this page are synthetic planning examples, not observed supplier failures or measured results. Timings, frequency caps and exit windows are proposed test inputs, not delivery or suppression guarantees. These calculations hold product cost fixed: contribution equals AOV × (margin − discount), and contribution lost equals AOV × discount; fees, shipping and returns are excluded. SMS eligibility requires current purpose-specific permission and applicable local-law review; the repeated TCPA shorthand is not a complete consent rule.</p>
      <p>Assign one migration owner and one sending owner for each channel and recovery purpose. Map store, customer, checkout and order identifiers before importing events; do not merge people by a shared email address alone. Preserve permission evidence by channel and purpose, including its source, timestamp and withdrawal state; an export is not fresh permission. Reconcile current purchases, cancellations, inventory and suppressions before enrollment and again before each proposed send. Assign each queued message to one active system, cancel or reconcile old queues before enabling replacements, and document collision rules across welcome, recovery and SMS flows. For replayed or delayed events, use a stable event identifier and an idempotency record scoped to the store, purpose and intended action; record processed events and test duplicate and out-of-order delivery without sending to customers. Keep recovery-message ownership separate from revenue attribution, and reconcile both against actual orders.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Export consent + suppress recent purchasers</li>
        <li>Rebuild abandoned cart with holdout cohort</li>
        <li>Document collision rules with welcome/SMS</li>
        <li>Inventory-aware suppressions before discounts</li>
        <li>Margin review at day 30 post-cutover</li>
      </ul>
